# Eve Smart Mirror AI Stack

## Overview

**Eve** is a local, voice-first smart mirror assistant intended to replace a traditional smart speaker while adding a visual dashboard and smart-home control.

---

# Repository Contents

```text
EveMirror/
│
├── wake_word_training/       — Hey Eve ONNX model training pipeline
│   ├── train.py
│   ├── generate_clips_piper.py
│   ├── test_wakeword.py
│   ├── my_model.yaml
│   ├── openwakeword/         — vendored openWakeWord library
│   ├── piper-sample-generator/
│   └── my_custom_model/
│       └── hey_eve.onnx
│
├── mirror_client/            — thin client: wake detection, VAD, audio, WebSocket
│   ├── listen.py             — Phase 2: wake → chime → VAD record → command.wav
│   ├── runtime.py            — 24/7 supervisor for listener + voice loop
│   └── requirements.txt
│
├── eve_agent/                — AI server: agent, Ollama client, MCP client, TTS
│   ├── transcribe.py         — Phase 3: command.wav → transcribed text (faster-whisper)
│   └── requirements.txt
│
├── dashboard/                — React + TypeScript kiosk UI
│
└── mcp_servers/              — MCP tool servers (test, Home Assistant, etc.)
```

Core voice pipeline, agent, weather tool routing/cache, dashboard rendering, and debug utilities are implemented and actively evolving.

---

# Current Implementation Flow (As Built)

This section documents what is actually wired together and running today. It is
more granular than the conceptual "Interaction Flow" and planned architecture
sections further down this document, which describe where the project is headed
(real MCP servers, model-initiated tool calls, Home Assistant, etc.). See
"Tool routing is deterministic, not model-driven" below for a concrete note on
the current vs. planned tool-calling model.

## Process topology — who starts what

`mirror_client/runtime.py` is the supervisor used for always-on operation. It
spawns and auto-restarts three independent OS processes and gates the voice
worker on an Ollama health check:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    R["runtime.py (supervisor)"] -->|spawn, immediately| ES["dashboard_event_server.py<br/>WS ws://127.0.0.1:8765<br/>UDP ingest 127.0.0.1:8766"]
    R -->|spawn, immediately| L["listen.py<br/>wake word + VAD"]
    R -->|poll GET /api/tags every 1s| H{Ollama reachable?}
    H -->|no| H
    H -->|yes| VL["voice_loop.py --watch"]
    L -->|writes WAV on end of speech| WAV[("mirror_client/recordings/*.wav")]
    VL -->|polls dir every 0.5s| WAV

    R -. "monitors + restarts on crash<br/>(3s backoff)" .-> ES
    R -. restarts .-> L
    R -. restarts .-> VL
```

`eve_agent/agent.py` is a separate, independent entry point: running it directly
with `--text`/`--audio`/interactively exercises the same tooling → Ollama → TTS →
dashboard-event path described below, but without the mic/wake-word pipeline or
the `runtime.py` supervisor.

## One full voice turn, end to end

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Mic
    participant listen as listen.py
    participant Disk as recordings/*.wav
    participant VL as voice_loop.py
    participant STT as transcribe.py (faster-whisper)
    participant Tool as tooling.maybe_call_tools()
    participant Agent as agent.py (build_messages/ask_ollama)
    participant Ollama
    participant TTS as tts_local.py (Piper)
    participant UDP as dashboard_events.emit_event()
    participant WS as dashboard_event_server.py
    participant UI as Dashboard (App.tsx)

    Mic->>listen: continuous 16kHz audio
    listen->>listen: openWakeWord scores each 80ms window
    listen->>listen: "Hey Eve" >= threshold -> play chime
    listen->>listen: Silero VAD records until trailing silence/15s cap
    listen->>Disk: write command_<timestamp>.wav
    VL->>Disk: detect new file (poll loop)
    VL->>UDP: state_change TRANSCRIBING
    VL->>STT: transcribe(wav)
    STT-->>VL: user text
    VL->>UDP: user_transcript {text}
    VL->>Tool: should_enable_thinking(text)
    VL->>UDP: state_change THINKING {think}
    VL->>Agent: build_messages(base_messages, text)
    Agent->>Tool: maybe_call_tools(text)
    Note over Tool: deterministic keyword/regex routing;<br/>executes weather/time/backlight synchronously<br/>(real HTTP + real serial writes), *before* the model runs
    Tool-->>Agent: notes[] + payload{} (weather, time, backlight, smart_home_attempt stub)
    Agent->>Agent: append payload as "trusted local tool context" to the user message
    Agent->>Ollama: POST /api/chat {model, messages, think}
    Ollama-->>Agent: natural-language reply (narrates the pre-fetched context)
    VL->>UDP: assistant_response {text}
    VL->>TTS: speak(reply)
    TTS->>UDP: subtitle_prepare / subtitle_progress / tts_audio_level / tts_audio_bands / subtitle_done
    TTS-->>Mic: audio out (speakers)
    VL->>UDP: state_change IDLE
    UDP->>WS: one UDP JSON datagram per emit_event() call
    WS->>UI: re-broadcast same JSON to every connected WebSocket client
    UI->>UI: handleEvent() drives activity/animation, subtitles, weather tile
```

## Tool routing is deterministic, not model-driven

`tooling.maybe_call_tools(user_text)` is plain Python keyword/regex matching run
**before** the Ollama call — Qwen is never given a tool/function-calling schema
(`ask_ollama`'s request body is just `{model, messages, stream, think}`) and
never decides which tool to invoke. The agent decides, executes the action, and
hands the model a pre-computed result to narrate:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    T[maybe_call_tools text] --> W{weather keywords?}
    W -->|yes| GW["get_current_weather()<br/>real Open-Meteo HTTP call + JSON disk cache"]
    T --> TM{time/date keywords?}
    TM -->|yes| GT[get_current_time]
    T --> BL{backlight/screen-brightness keywords?}
    BL -->|yes| RB["resolve_backlight_target_percent()"] --> SB["set_backlight_brightness()<br/>real serial write to Feather over USB<br/>(firmware clamps 0-100% to 0-2V DAC)"]
    T --> SH{other light/thermostat/garage/alarm keywords?}
    SH -->|yes| STUB["smart_home_attempt stub<br/>(status=attempted_not_executed, no device call)"]
    GW --> P[notes + payload]
    GT --> P
    SB --> P
    STUB --> P
    P -->|merged into user message as trusted context| Agent[agent.build_messages]
```

`mcp_servers/test_server.py` mirrors a few of these as `*_tool()` functions in an
MCP-shaped module, but nothing in `agent.py` or `voice_loop.py` imports or runs
it — it is unused scaffolding for the real MCP integration described in the
"MCP Architecture" and "Phase 5 — Test MCP Server" sections below, not part of
the live request path today.

## Dashboard event delivery

All runtime state (transcripts, replies, subtitles, TTS audio levels, weather
tile data, layout changes) reaches the browser through one shared relay:

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    E1[agent.py / voice_loop.py] -->|emit_event type,payload| U[(UDP 127.0.0.1:8766)]
    E2[tts_local.py] -->|emit_event| U
    U --> S[dashboard_event_server.py]
    S -->|broadcast JSON| C1[Dashboard tab 1]
    S -->|broadcast JSON| C2[Dashboard tab N]
    C1 -->|dashboard_refresh_weather over WS| S
    S -->|fetch| GW2[tooling.get_current_weather]
    GW2 -->|tool_context envelope| S
```

`dashboard_event_server.py` is the only component that listens on both sides: it
re-broadcasts anything it receives over UDP to every connected WebSocket client,
and it is also the sole handler for one inbound WS message type
(`dashboard_refresh_weather`, used by the dashboard's manual weather-refresh
button), which it turns into a fresh `tool_context` broadcast.

## Entry points at a glance

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `python mirror_client/runtime.py` | Supervises listener + event server + voice loop forever, with crash auto-restart. Primary "always on" entry point. |
| `python mirror_client/listen.py` | Wake word + VAD capture only; writes WAV files. Started by `runtime.py`, or run standalone for mic tuning. |
| `python eve_agent/voice_loop.py --watch` | Watches `recordings/` and runs each new WAV through STT → tools → Ollama → TTS → dashboard events. Started by `runtime.py`. |
| `python eve_agent/voice_loop.py --audio <wav>` | Same pipeline, one specific WAV, no watching. |
| `python eve_agent/agent.py --text "..."` / `--audio <wav>` / interactive | Same tooling/Ollama/TTS/event path as `voice_loop.py`, entered directly with typed text or a specific recording — no mic, no wake word, no `runtime.py`. |
| `python eve_agent/dashboard_event_server.py` | Standalone WS (`:8765`) + UDP (`:8766`) relay. Required for the dashboard to show any live state; started by `runtime.py`. |
| `python eve_agent/backlight_control.py <0-100>` | Manually sets the mirror backlight brightness directly over serial, independent of Ollama/the agent — for testing the Feather/panel without the model running. |

---

# Startup Commands (Current Dev Workflow)

Run commands from the repository root unless noted.

## 1) Activate the Python environment

Windows PowerShell:

```powershell
(Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope Process -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned) ; (& .\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1)
```

## 2) Start Ollama

```powershell
ollama serve
```

## 3) Start the dashboard (Vite)

```powershell
cd .\dashboard
npm run dev
```

Optional dashboard build check:

```powershell
cd .\dashboard
npm run build
```

## 4) Start the 24/7 mirror runtime supervisor

This launches/monitors listener, event server, and voice loop.

```powershell
python .\mirror_client\runtime.py
```

Useful runtime variants:

```powershell
python .\mirror_client\runtime.py --device 1
python .\mirror_client\runtime.py --tts-speaker 64
python .\mirror_client\runtime.py --no-tts
```

## 5) Run the text agent directly (single turn or interactive)

Single-turn text:

```powershell
python .\eve_agent\agent.py --text "whats the weather like"
```

Single-turn from audio:

```powershell
python .\eve_agent\agent.py --audio .\mirror_client\recordings\command_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS.wav
```

Interactive mode:

```powershell
python .\eve_agent\agent.py
```

---

# Dashboard Debug Menu Tools

The dashboard has two debug panels that can be enabled independently.

## Layout Debug panel

Enable either way:

```powershell
# Option A: env var before starting Vite
$env:VITE_SHOW_LAYOUT_DEBUG_UI="1"
npm run dev
```

or query string:

```text
http://localhost:5173/?layoutDebug=1
```

Tools currently available:

- `Default Placement` button:
    - returns to the default animation/tools placement
    - dismisses active tiles through the transition choreography
- `Tile Preset` dropdown:
    - `No Tile`
    - `Animation Only`
    - `Large Default Tile`
    - `Large Weather Tile`
    - `3 Blank Tiles (Side by Side)`
    - `4 Blank Tiles (2 x 2)`
    - `2 Blank Tiles`

## Animation TTS Test panel

Enable either way:

```powershell
# Option A: env var before starting Vite
$env:VITE_SHOW_TTS_TEST_UI="1"
npm run dev
```

or query string:

```text
http://localhost:5173/?ttsTest=1
```

Tools currently available:

- `Speak Test`:
    - emits subtitle prep/progress/done events for a typed phrase
    - exercises speaking-state animation timing
- `Stop`:
    - stops active test speech timeline

## Weather debug behavior currently in place

- Weather tool context events are emitted to dashboard (`tool_context`) during agent runs.
- Last real weather payload is persisted in browser local storage for UI continuity.
- Backend weather cache persists to JSON at `eve_agent/weather_cache.json` and is refreshed periodically.
- Same-day hourly history is retained through day rollover for graph continuity.

---

# Interaction Flow

Primary interaction flow:

```text
User says:
"Hey Eve"

        ↓

Wake-word detection

        ↓

User speaks a command/question

        ↓

Speech-to-text

        ↓

Eve agent/orchestrator

        ↓

Local Qwen model through Ollama

        ↓

MCP / tool calls as needed

        ↓

Natural-language response
+
dashboard action
+
optional smart-home action

        ↓

Text-to-speech
```

Example:

```text
User:
"Hey Eve"

Eve:
*activation chime*

User:
"Is it cold outside?"

Eve:
→ detects weather intent
→ uses weather tool
→ receives current temperature
→ responds without extended reasoning
→ shows weather on dashboard

Eve:
"Yeah, it's pretty cold. It's 30°F and feels like 24°F."
```

