# Wake Word

Wake phrase:

```text
Hey Eve
```

Technology:

```text
openWakeWord
```

A custom:

```text
hey_eve.onnx
```

model has already been trained and tested successfully using the scripts in `wake_word_training/`.

Current Windows development environment (Eve runtime):

```text
Python 3.11.9

C:\...\Eve             ← local dev path, not committed to repo
│
├── .venv
├── models
│   └── hey_eve.onnx
└── openWakeWord
```

Wake-word detection should run continuously on the mirror thin client.

---

# Wake State

Normal state:

```text
WAITING_FOR_WAKE
```

When:

```text
"Hey Eve"
```

is detected:

```text
WAKE DETECTED
      ↓
play acknowledgement chime
      ↓
LISTENING
```

---

# Voice Activity Detection

Planned VAD:

```text
Silero VAD
```

Purpose:

```text
detect when speech begins
detect when speech ends
avoid fixed recording durations
```

Example:

```text
"Hey Eve"
      ↓
*dink*
      ↓
LISTEN

"What's the weather tomorrow?"

      ↓
speech ends
      ↓
~500-800 ms silence
      ↓
stop recording
```

The silence threshold will be tuned during testing.

---

# Speech-to-Text

Planned STT:

```text
faster-whisper
```

Likely model:

```text
Whisper large-v3-turbo
```

This should run on the AI server rather than the mirror client.

The mirror sends recorded speech over the LAN.

Example:

```text
Mirror microphone
      ↓
WAV/audio stream
      ↓
AI server
      ↓
faster-whisper
      ↓
"Is it cold outside?"
```

---

# Text-to-Speech

Initial TTS target:

```text
Kokoro-82M
```

Purpose:

```text
Qwen text
    ↓
Kokoro
    ↓
audio
    ↓
mirror speakers
```

Piper is a possible lightweight alternative.

TTS will initially run on the AI server.

