# Dashboard

The dashboard should be a web application.

Recommended stack:

```text
React
TypeScript
Chromium kiosk
WebSockets
```

The mirror launches Chromium fullscreen automatically.

Example dashboard capabilities:

```text
weather
calendar
clock
music
Home Assistant state
timers
notifications
search results
```

---

# Dashboard Tool Calls

Qwen should control the dashboard through structured tools instead of mouse/vision control.

Examples:

```text
dashboard.show_weather()

dashboard.show_calendar()

dashboard.show_music()

dashboard.show_home()

dashboard.set_layout(
    left="calendar",
    right="weather"
)
```

The agent translates these into WebSocket messages.

Example:

```json
{
  "event": "show_widget",
  "widget": "weather"
}
```

The React dashboard responds immediately.

---

# Automatic UI Behavior

Some dashboard behavior can occur automatically based on tool usage.

Example:

```text
User:
"Is it cold outside?"

Qwen:
weather.current()

        ↓

Agent sees weather tool was used

        ↓

automatically display weather widget
```

This avoids requiring Qwen to issue redundant dashboard commands for obvious cases.

Explicit requests still work:

```text
"Show me the full weather forecast."
```

can trigger a full weather view.

---

# Web Search

The model should not directly access the internet.

Instead Eve will receive a search tool.

Possible implementations:

```text
SearXNG
search API
custom web-search MCP server
```

Example:

```text
User:
"When is the next SpaceX launch?"

        ↓

Qwen recognizes current information is needed

        ↓

web.search(...)

        ↓

search results

        ↓

Qwen summarizes them
```

This lets the underlying LLM remain completely local while still retrieving current information when requested.

---

# Presence and Display Control

Presence detection should remain simple and separate from the LLM.

Possible sensor:

```text
mmWave presence sensor
```

Behavior:

```text
active hours?
     ↓
yes
     ↓
person detected?
     ↓
yes
     ↓
LCD on
```

After a configurable period without presence:

```text
LCD off
```

No large computer-vision model is planned.

---

# Mirror Runtime States

Recommended state machine:

```text
NIGHT
│
│ wake functions limited
│ display off
│
▼

IDLE
│
│ wake-word detection active
│ presence monitoring active
│ CPU mostly idle
│
▼

LISTENING
│
│ VAD active
│ microphone recording
│
▼

PROCESSING
│
│ audio sent to AI server
│ dashboard indicates activity
│
▼

SPEAKING
│
│ Eve response playing
│ dashboard updated
│
▼

SESSION_IDLE
│
│ recent conversation retained
│ waiting for follow-up
│
▼

TIMEOUT
│
│ conversation deleted
│
▼

IDLE
```

---

# Follow-Up Conversation Behavior

During an active session, the user should not necessarily have to repeat:

```text
"Hey Eve"
```

Example:

```text
User:
"Hey Eve"

User:
"What's the weather today?"

Eve:
"It's 65 degrees and cloudy."

User:
"What about tomorrow?"

Eve:
"Tomorrow should be warmer..."
```

After approximately:

```text
30-60 seconds
```

of inactivity:

```text
session ends
conversation.clear()
```

Then the next interaction requires:

```text
"Hey Eve"
```

again.

---

# Security Model

The LLM proposes actions.

The Eve Agent authorizes and executes them.

```text
Qwen:
"I want to call set_light(...)"

        ↓

Eve Agent:
Is this tool allowed?
Are the arguments valid?
Does this require confirmation?

        ↓

MCP Client

        ↓

MCP Server
```

Different tools can have different permission levels.

### Low-Risk

```text
weather lookup
time lookup
calendar read
lights
music
dashboard
volume
```

Generally execute immediately.

### Higher-Risk

```text
unlock door
open garage
disable alarm
delete calendar item
send messages
make purchases
```

Require explicit confirmation or additional authorization.

The model itself should never receive raw service credentials.

